
======================================================================================
1. Write cost vs. list breadth (normalized entities)
======================================================================================

  writeQuery into a list of n normalized entities
         n          cold    scale     identical    scale     1 changed    scale
  -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
       100       1.50 ms        -      796.1 us        -      757.4 us        -
      1000       8.79 ms    0.59n       7.75 ms    0.97n       7.62 ms    1.01n
      5000      44.00 ms    1.00n      39.65 ms    1.02n      39.68 ms    1.04n
     20000     182.46 ms    1.04n     164.33 ms    1.04n     166.83 ms    1.05n
  (scale = growth factor divided by the size ratio: 1.00n = linear, 2.00n = quadratic)

  Reading: "identical" still pays the full traversal + normalization + deep
  equality; it only avoids dirtying fields. There is no early-out for an
  unchanged payload, because the writer cannot know it is unchanged until it
  has normalized it.

  Why the n=100 "cold" cell above is disproportionately high (it drags the
  next row's scale well below 1.00n). Re-measuring the same three writes here,
  after the process has warmed up:
    write cold n=100, as measured first : 1.50 ms
    brand-new cache, 100 new entities   : 874.5 us
    primed EMPTY cache, 100 new         : 840.4 us
    overwrite of 100 existing           : 717.6 us
  Two separate effects, neither of them per-entity work:
    1. JIT. The table's cold n=100 is the FIRST measurement in the process;
       the identical operation re-measured here is cheaper.
    2. One-time per-cache setup (document transform, type policies, fresh
       StoreReader/StoreWriter): the gap between "brand-new" and "primed".
  With both removed, CREATING n entities and OVERWRITING n identical ones cost
  the same: the dirtying a creation does is worth about the same as the equal()
  calls an overwrite does. Per-entity write cost does not depend on whether the
  entity already existed.

======================================================================================
2. Read cost vs. list breadth: cold, warm, and after one dirty field
======================================================================================

  readQuery over a list of n normalized entities
         n          cold    scale          warm    scale after 1 dirty    scale
  -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
       100       1.56 ms        -        2.9 us        -      186.9 us        -
      1000      15.70 ms    1.01n        2.5 us    0.09n       1.66 ms    0.89n
      5000      78.63 ms    1.00n        2.7 us    0.21n       8.87 ms    1.07n
     20000     333.45 ms    1.06n        2.5 us    0.23n      45.08 ms    1.27n
  (scale = growth factor divided by the size ratio: 1.00n = linear, 2.00n = quadratic)

  Note that "after 1 dirty" is still LINEAR in n, roughly 8x cheaper than a cold
  read. Only three memo entries re-execute (the entity, the array, the root), but
  re-executing the array entry means n canRead calls plus n memoized
  executeSelectionSet lookups. Entries-recomputed and work-done are not the same.

  Structure sharing after modifying 1 of 500 entities:
    identical (===) array elements reused: 499/500
    top-level result object reused:        false
    feed array reused:                     false
  The parent array is rebuilt (it is one memo entry) but every untouched
  element object is reused by reference. That is what keeps React re-renders
  proportional to what actually changed.

  Memo entries retained by a single read (bounded by cacheSizes limits
  50 000 / 10 000 respectively):
    n=  100  executeSelectionSet=   101  executeSubSelectedArray=1
    n= 1000  executeSelectionSet=  1001  executeSubSelectedArray=1
    n= 5000  executeSelectionSet=  5001  executeSubSelectedArray=1

======================================================================================
3. Normalized vs. embedded (untyped) payloads of the same size
======================================================================================

  n entities, 6 scalar fields each
         n    write norm    scale   write embed    scale     read norm    scale    read embed    scale
  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
       100      873.8 us        -      711.0 us        -        2.9 us        -        3.0 us        -
      1000       8.14 ms    0.93n       5.86 ms    0.82n        2.5 us    0.09n        2.5 us    0.08n
      5000      44.10 ms    1.08n      28.91 ms    0.99n        2.6 us    0.21n        2.6 us    0.21n
  (scale = growth factor divided by the size ratio: 1.00n = linear, 2.00n = quadratic)

  Store entry counts for n=2000:
    normalized: 2001 entries (1 root + n entities)
    embedded:   1 entries (root only — the whole list lives in one field)

  Embedded payloads write faster (no identify, no reference indirection) and
  read faster warm, but they are a single cache field: changing one element
  dirties the entire list, and nothing is shared with any other query.

======================================================================================
4. Depth: cost per level of nesting
======================================================================================

  a single chain of d nested entities
         n    write norm    scale   write embed    scale     read cold    scale     read warm    scale    deep dirty    scale
  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
         4       68.2 us        -       48.1 us        -       74.4 us        -        2.8 us        -       71.7 us        -
        16      170.5 us    0.63n      132.0 us    0.69n      206.4 us    0.69n        2.7 us    0.24n      220.6 us    0.77n
        64      610.2 us    0.89n      448.5 us    0.85n      737.3 us    0.89n        2.4 us    0.22n       1.26 ms    1.43n
       128       1.15 ms    0.94n      764.3 us    0.85n       1.37 ms    0.93n        2.4 us    0.50n       3.45 ms    1.37n
  (scale = growth factor divided by the size ratio: 1.00n = linear, 2.00n = quadratic)

  "deep dirty" is the headline number: modifying the LEAF of a d-deep chain marks
  every ancestor memo entry DirtyChild, and re-executing the root walks back down
  re-verifying each level. Note its scale column climbs to ~1.4 (superlinear) while
  "read cold" stays ~0.9 (linear), and that from d=16 upward the re-read already
  costs MORE than a cold read of the whole chain — the memo graph is a net cost
  here, and the gap widens with depth. Depth turns a point mutation into a
  root-to-leaf recomputation. Breadth does not — see section 2.

======================================================================================
5. Nested arrays: outer x inner
======================================================================================

  outer groups each holding inner normalized rows
         n         write    scale     read cold    scale     read warm    scale   1 row dirty    scale
  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
     10x10      596.1 us        -      838.1 us        -        2.6 us        -       78.1 us        -
    10x100       4.52 ms    0.76n       8.62 ms    1.03n        2.3 us    0.09n      305.5 us    0.39n
   100x100      47.74 ms    1.06n      88.20 ms    1.02n        2.1 us    0.09n      569.5 us    0.19n
   100x500     251.86 ms    1.06n     472.01 ms    1.07n       2.21 ms  207.39n       2.27 ms    0.80n
  (scale = growth factor divided by the size ratio: 1.00n = linear, 2.00n = quadratic)

  Watch the "read warm" column at 100x500. Every other row is ~2 us; that one
  jumps by three orders of magnitude. 100*500 rows + 100 groups + ROOT_QUERY =
  50101 entities, just over the 50 000 executeSelectionSet limit, so the read
  evicts its own memo entries as it goes. This is the LRU cliff of section 9
  reached by accident, from a shape that looks unremarkable.

  arrays of arrays of plain scalars (no entities, no selection set)
         n         write    scale     read warm    scale
  ------------------------------------------------------
    10x100        6.8 us        -        2.2 us        -
   100x100        7.0 us    0.10n        2.1 us    0.10n
  100x1000        6.7 us    0.10n        2.1 us    0.10n
  (scale = growth factor divided by the size ratio: 1.00n = linear, 2.00n = quadratic)

  A scalar array without a sub-selection is stored and returned as ONE field
  value. It costs a deep-equality pass on write and nothing on read, but it is
  also atomic: any change replaces the whole array.

======================================================================================
6. Broadcast cost vs. number of watchers
======================================================================================

  one write with w watchers registered on the same query
         n      relevant    scale     unrelated    scale
  ------------------------------------------------------
         1      22.57 ms        -      101.4 us        -
        10      22.18 ms    0.10n      136.4 us    0.13n
        50      22.28 ms    0.20n      273.7 us    0.40n
       200      23.20 ms    0.26n      695.9 us    0.64n
  (scale = growth factor divided by the size ratio: 1.00n = linear, 2.00n = quadratic)

  "unrelated" is the equality-gate path: the watches are not dirty, so
  maybeBroadcastWatch returns its memoized value and no diff is recomputed.
  That is the difference between O(w) cheap checks and O(w) full re-reads.

  50 watchers on the SAME document:      22.78 ms
  50 watchers on 50 DISTINCT documents:  3.37 s
  Ratio: 148.1x. Memo entries are keyed by selection-set
  NODE identity, so structurally identical but separately-parsed documents
  share nothing. This is why DocumentTransform's WeakCache matters.

======================================================================================
7. Transactions, optimistic layers, and layer depth
======================================================================================

  k stacked optimistic layers over a 2000-entity store
         n    add+remove    scale  read through    scale remove bottom    scale   unwind LIFO    scale   unwind FIFO    scale
  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
         1       77.8 us        -        7.8 us        -        6.3 us        -        6.3 us        -        8.1 us        -
         4      198.1 us    0.64n        6.3 us    0.20n       46.0 us    1.82n       10.4 us    0.41n       92.9 us    2.86n
        16       1.67 ms    2.11n        6.7 us    0.26n      182.1 us    0.99n       27.0 us    0.65n       1.42 ms    3.82n
        64      25.48 ms    3.81n        7.4 us    0.28n      734.8 us    1.01n       81.9 us    0.76n      24.43 ms    4.30n
  (scale = growth factor divided by the size ratio: 1.00n = linear, 2.00n = quadratic)

  "remove bottom" is the expensive one: removing a layer that is not on top
  replays every layer above it (EntityStore.removeLayer -> Layer.newLayer +
  replay), so the cost is proportional to the number of layers above it times
  the size of each layer's write.

  Compare the last two columns: unwinding the SAME stack costs O(k) popping from
  the top and O(k^2) removing from the bottom, because every FIFO removal replays
  the layers above it. "add+remove" uses the FIFO order, which is why it inherits
  the same quadratic scale column.

  "read through" is FLAT in k, and that is not a contradiction: the read is warm,
  so it is a memo hit at the top of the chain and never walks the layers at all.
  The O(k) lookup chain is only paid on a memo MISS.

  100 writes, 1 watcher on a 2000-entity list:
    unbatched: 5.42 ms
    batched:   1.54 ms   (3.5x faster)
  The saving is entirely the avoided re-reads: each broadcast recomputes the
  watcher's diff, and a diff over the full list is the dominant term.

======================================================================================
8. Optimistic reads maintain a SECOND set of memo entries
======================================================================================

  InMemoryCache.init() sets this.optimisticData = rootStore.stump, NOT this.data:
    optimisticData === data      : false
    optimisticData constructor   : Stump
    groups are the same object   : false
    optimistic group's parent    : the root group

  executeSelectionSet memo entries, 2000-entity list, ZERO optimistic layers:
    after write                  : 0
    after optimistic:false diff  : 2001
    after optimistic:true  diff  : 4002   (+2001 new)
    root result === optimistic result : false
  Every watched query therefore costs TWO full sets of memo entries, because
  ObservableQuery always watches with optimistic: true while readQuery and
  readFragment default to optimistic: false. Budget memo capacity accordingly.

  first optimistic diff after a warm root read: 52.77 ms
  warm optimistic diff:                        1.7 us
  A warm root read buys the optimistic read nothing.

======================================================================================
9. The memo LRU cliff (executeSelectionSet max = 50 000)
======================================================================================

  Warm read cost as the entity count crosses the memo limit
   entities  memo entries     warm read  over limit?
  ----------------------------------------------------
      10101         10101        1.8 us           no
      40101         40101        1.7 us           no
      49101         49101        1.7 us           no
      50101         50000       2.17 ms          YES
      60101         50000     116.95 ms          YES

  A single query whose result contains more entities than the memo can hold
  evicts its own entries while it reads. Every subsequent "warm" read is then
  a cold read, and the cliff is abrupt rather than gradual: below the limit the
  read is microseconds, above it milliseconds. Raise it with
  cacheSizes["inMemoryCache.executeSelectionSet"], or do not read that many
  entities in one query.

