10. Field-key construction: arguments and canonicalStringify
======================================================================================

  one field with k arguments over a 50-item result
         n         write    scale     read cold    scale
  ------------------------------------------------------
         0      304.5 us        -      417.1 us        -
         2      274.6 us    0.45n      456.6 us    0.55n
         8      259.1 us    0.24n      375.5 us    0.21n
        24      305.0 us    0.39n      441.0 us    0.39n
  (scale = growth factor divided by the size ratio: 1.00n = linear, 2.00n = quadratic)

  one field whose argument is an object nested d levels deep (1-item result)
         n         write    scale    fresh vars    scale
  ------------------------------------------------------
         1       18.0 us        -       17.0 us        -
         8       23.9 us    0.17n       24.9 us    0.18n
        32       49.1 us    0.51n       53.5 us    0.54n
       128      150.3 us    0.77n      154.3 us    0.72n
  (scale = growth factor divided by the size ratio: 1.00n = linear, 2.00n = quadratic)

  Resulting store field key (note the sorted, fully-serialized args):
    search({"filter":{"a":2,"nested":{"a":2,"nested":{"a":2,"m":3,"z":1},"z":1},"z":1}})

======================================================================================
11. keyFields: identity extraction cost
======================================================================================

  Book list write cost by keyFields configuration
  config                                 write   vs default
  ----------------------------------------------------------
  default (__typename + id)           21.64 ms        1.00x
  keyFields: ['isbn']                 27.94 ms        1.29x
  keyFields: 3 flat fields            29.76 ms        1.37x
  keyFields with nested path          30.88 ms        1.43x
  keyFields: false (embedded)         21.69 ms        1.00x

  Every normalizable object pays identify() on write. A nested keyFields path
  is the most expensive configuration: extractKeyPath walks into the sub-object
  and canonically stringifies it for every entity. Note that keyFields: false is
  NOT free — it removes the identify cost but turns the whole list into one
  embedded field value, which the writer must deep-compare on every write.

======================================================================================
12. Eviction, garbage collection and extract
======================================================================================

  lifecycle operations over a store of n entities
         n  evict entity    scale   evict field    scale       gc noop    scale    gc collect    scale       extract    scale       restore    scale
  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      1000       22.1 us        -       20.7 us        -       1.03 ms        -       3.91 ms        -      174.6 us        -       1.54 ms        -
      5000       21.1 us    0.19n       21.7 us    0.21n       5.16 ms    1.00n      19.93 ms    1.02n       1.20 ms    1.38n       8.89 ms    1.15n
     20000       22.5 us    0.27n       23.2 us    0.27n      27.41 ms    1.33n      83.55 ms    1.05n       6.04 ms    1.25n      37.66 ms    1.06n
  (scale = growth factor divided by the size ratio: 1.00n = linear, 2.00n = quadratic)

  gc() is a full mark-and-sweep: it walks every root and every reachable field
  of every entity, so it is O(store) EVEN WHEN IT COLLECTS NOTHING. restore()
  is dramatically cheaper than a write of the same data because it skips
  normalization entirely — the snapshot is already normalized.

======================================================================================
13. Result caching off: what memoization is worth
======================================================================================

  n=5000 entities:
    read warm,  resultCaching: true   1.8 us
    read warm,  resultCaching: false  28.33 ms   (16049x slower)
    write,      resultCaching: true   45.07 ms
    write,      resultCaching: false  39.54 ms   (0.88x)
  Memoization is a read-path optimization paid for on the write path through
  dependency bookkeeping. The read-side win is orders of magnitude; the
  write-side cost is a modest constant factor.

======================================================================================
14. Development-build overhead (maybeDeepFreeze + warnAboutDataLoss)
======================================================================================

  n=5000 entities, production build vs. development build:
    write     prod   45.01 ms   dev   45.50 ms   1.01x
    read cold prod   79.76 ms   dev   88.21 ms   1.11x
    results frozen: prod=false dev=true
  The development build deep-freezes every object it returns and runs
  warnAboutDataLoss on every write. The overhead is real but modest on this
  shape, because maybeDeepFreeze short-circuits on already-frozen subtrees
  and the reader reuses frozen memo entries. It grows with the proportion of
  freshly-created result objects, so it is worst on cold reads.

